1. INTRODUCTION


     CCtest, a bi-directional copy-and-compare test utility that runs
under both DOS and OS/2, will copy a file to a destination and make a
file comparision.  This utility was created to improve the existing
"fctest" by allowing different file sizes and file content.  CCtest
allows users to choose any number for file sizes as well as any data
pattern to be used in the test.  Also, CCtest can run in random
mode to randomly generate file content and file size to simulate the
real world activity.  Unlike "fctest", CCtest can copy a file to as
many destinations (network drives) as you like(Max. 30).  This gives
users the ability to test multiple servers from a single workstation. 
Also, from the performance number shown for each destination, the 
condition of the networks and file servers under tested can be easily
identified.  CCtest will provide bi-directional copy-and-compare 
operation to make sure the under-tested networks work correctly on 
both directions.




2.  CCTEST SYNTAX.


    CCtest <machine ID>  -switches (NOTE: the switches are case sensitive.)

    <Local machine ID> is the name of your local computer.  CCtest will use
                       this name to store the the file that will be copied to
                       other machines. When the file is copied back from the
                       destination, a new file with the same name but with
                       file extension ".bac" will be created to store the
                       coming-back file.  It is the user's responsibility
                       to ensure the machine ID is unique on all target
                       machines, especially when you run more than one CCtest 
                       in different screen group under OS/2.

    The switches are:
    -t<destination>    (Required)
                       <destination> is a list of drive names or UNC style
                       share names seperated by blank, comma or semicolon.
                       For example, -t d: e: f: \\server\sharename.  The
                       maximum number of characters in <destination> is 200
                       and the maximum number of destination that CCtest can
                       handle is 30.
                       "-t" switch is the only switch that has to be presented
                       when you invoke CCtest, all other switches are optional.


    -f<file name>      (Optional)
                       <file name> specifies the path and the name of the file
                       that you want to make copy-and-compare. When you use
                       this switch, you cannot use the other switch that used
                       to specify the file size or file content such as -p, -s,
                       -i and -r.  The maximum length of <file name> is 100
                       bytes.


    -s<file size>      (Optional)
                       <file size> is a decimal number like 100 or 20k, use
                       -s when you run CCtest using random file or pattern 
                       file. <file size> can be any number between 1 to 2**32.
                       NOTE: K=k=1024.

    -i<number>         (Optional)
                       Each time the <number> is added to the previous file
                       size to get a new file size for the next run of the 
                       test.  This switch needs to work with -s switch.  Like
                       -s, the increment number can be any number between 1 to
                       2**32.

    -r<number..number> (Optional)
                       "r" switch set the random number range that CCtest will
                       use as the file size, for example -r10k..500k.  The 
                       first number must be less than the second number and
                       both can be any number between 1 to 2**32.

    -b<number..number> (Optional)
                       "b" switch set the random number range that CCtest will
                       use as the buffer size in copying files. The default 
                       value is 1..1024, maximum buffer size is 20k.

    -p<file pattern>   (Optional)
                       Currently, CCtest supports only ASCII pattern. <file
                       pattern> is a string of ASCII characters with maximum
                       size of 100 characters. For example -pthis is a pattern.
                       To use '-' in the pattern, put two '-' instead (other-
                       wise CCtest will get confused with your '-' and 
                       swithes).

    -c                 (Optional)
                       Activate the CONTINUE mode.  In CONTINUE mode, CCtest
                       will not abort in case of a failure due to a file
                       compare error.  This switch is useful in over night 
                       or over the weekend test to gether failure 
                       information.

    -l<log file name>  (Optional)
                       CCtest will write statistics to "cctest.log" by default,
                       the -l switch allows users to name their own logfile.

    -D                 (Optional)
                       Activate the debugging mode, CCtest will be paused and
                       wait for a keyboard input between a copy and compare
                       operation.

    -L                 (Optional)
                       Disable the output to logfile for each pass and only
                       write failure infomation to logfile to save logfile
                       space in a over a long period of time CCtest.

    -C<comment>        (Optional)
                       <comment> will be displayed on the screen and logfile
                       to help the user remember the purpose of test.  Like
                       pattern switch (-p), use two '-' in your <comment>
                       where you want a '-'.  The maximum length of <comment>
                       is 50 bytes.

    -n<times>          (Optional)
                       Execute the copy and compare only <times> times.  By
                       default, CCtest will do copy-and-compare continuously
                       until some failure occurs.

    -N<cycles>         (Optional)
                       Reset all the options number to its origin and repeat
                       the CCtest <cycles> cycles.  For example:

                            CCtest id -td:\ -n10 -N3 -s10k -i1k
                       
                       This will cause CCtest to copy and compare a random
                       file using file size from 10k and increase 1k each time
                       pass until 10 times(-n10) limit is reached, the above
                       opertion will be repeat 3(-N3) cycles.

    -v                 (Optional)
                       -v switch disable all the VIO output to screen. 
    
    -S                 (Optional)
                       -S will stop option file if any file-compared error
                       happened. By default, CCtest will continue the options
                       file processing even error happened on the last 
                       options input.

                       [Note] -c will make -S inactive.

    -P                 (Optional)
                       -P switch will tell CCtest to send a copy of the error
                       information to PRN: when copy and compare failed.

    -z<time in seconds to sleep> (Optional)
                       This switch will put CCtest into sleep between 
                       copy-and-compares.

    -o<options file>   (Optional)
                       You can put all the options in a file and then invoke
                       CCtest by using -o<options file>, for example:
                       "CCtest machine_id -omyfile".  The following is a 
                       sample options file:
     
                       ; this is a comment.
                       * comment, too.
                       -t \\server\dir -n10
                       ;
                       ; invoke cctest again.
                       ;
                       -t d: e: \\server\dir -Cthis is a comment
                       ;
                       ; Use '+' to extend the current line.
                       ;
                       -C third test -t\\server\share +
                       -b1..200 -r1..1000k            +
                       -c -lnewlog



    The default setting are:

       -- File Size:             random (1 to 128k)
       -- Buffer Size:           random (1 to 1024)
       -- File Content:          random
       -- Break/Continue mode:   break
       -- Logfile name:          cctest.log
       -- Times:                 infinite
       -- Cycles:                1
  
    CCtest can run under both DOS and OS/2.  Also, CCtest doesn't prevent
    you from running more than one CCtest in different screen group under
    OS/2.



3. HOW TO RUN CCTEST - SOME EXAMPLES.


    1.  CCtest cp999.001 -t f: g:
        -- copy file to drive f: and g:, make comparision, then copy it back,
           make comparision again.  File content and size are randomly
           generated by the program.  The copied file will be named cp999.001 
           in the test.  The file sizes will be 1 to 128K which are randomly 
           selected by the program.  CCtest will generate a new file with 
           random content and random file size in each run of the copy-and-
           compare.  File "cp999.bac" on the current directory represents
           the file being copied back from the destination.

    2.  CCtest cp077 -t f: g: h: i: j: k: l: m: -ftestfile 
        -- copy and compare file against drives f:, g:, h:, i:, j:, k:, l:,
           and m: using the file "testfile".

    3.  CCtest cp118 -t \\openxns\share1 -n100 -ftestfile
        -- copy and compare the file "testfile" against the server "\\open
           xns\share1" 100 times. After 100 times copy and compare, CCtest 
           will terminate.

    4.  CCtest cp018 -t f: g: -c -s32767 -i100
        -- copy file to drives f: and g: in CONTINUE mode, file size 
           starting from 32767 and increment by 100 each time the test passes,
           file content are randomly generated by the program.

    5.  CCtest cp999 -t x: y: -p1234567890 -s10k 
        -- copy file to drives x: and y:, file content is made of the pattern
           "1234567890", file size is 10k.

    6.  CCtest cp111 -o optfile.001
        -- invoke CCtest using options file "optfile.001".



4.  SCREEN OUTPUT AND LOG FILE.


    CCtest write to the screen the information about each file copy and
compare in the following format:

> \\server\dir    234561 bytes BufSize 155   112 Kb/sec WRITE  Compare...PASS
> \\server\longdi 234561 bytes BufSize 155    99 Kb/sec WRITE  Compare...PASS
< \\server\dir    234561 bytes BufSize 155   103 Kb/sec READ   Compare...PASS
< \\server\longdi 234561 bytes BufSize 155    95 Kb/sec READ   Compare...

The first character on each line (either a '>' or '<') represent the
direction of file copy, for '>', it means file is copied from where
you run CCtest to the destination that follows the '>' symbol.  After
the name of destination is a number that represents the file size,
the buffer size, and then the WRITE or READ performance number. 
CCtest also shows the total transfered size and average performance
number for each destination in a window on the upper side of screen.
Since you don't have a screen which is big enough to keep all the 
information, only the first ten destinations can be displayed on
this summary window.  CCtest will also write about the same statistic 
as on the screen to the logfile, the only difference is - for each time 
pass, a time stamp will be printed at the end of line.



5.  MISC. INFO.


   5.1 Use Ctl-C to abort CCtest when necessary, the control-C handler
       routine in CCtest will finish the current copy or compare 
       operation and write statistics of each destination to logfile
       before exit.

   5.2 Avoid to use the current directory as one of the destination, file open
       error will occur if that happen (you cannot copy a file to itself).

   5.3 Some of the switches are conflict with each other, for example -f and
       -p, you have to decide either to use a file or a pattern in the test,
       CCtest won't make that decision for you!  Also, -i switch needs -s
       switch to give a starting file size number in order to increase the
       file size number based on that.  In summary, CCtest will check all the 
       supplied switches and search for any conflict that is caused by the 
       following switch pairs:

              -f and -p
              -f and -r
              -s and -r
              -i and no -s
              -f and -s
       In case of any confict, the execution will be aborted by CCtest.

   5.4 In case of a file compare error, CCtest will show the name of source
       file and destination file, the user should use other utility like
       comp.exe or diff.exe to find out the problem.  Also, CCtest will inform
       users the errors due to file open, file read, and file write, it is
       the user's responsiblity to find out the reason why such errors will
       occur (network link drop, server die.....).


   5.5 If you choose an existing file on the disk to run the CCtest (using 
       -f<filename> switch), CCtest will creat a new file using the machine
       ID you provide in the command line parameters as the new file
       name (in order to maintaine the consistency of file naming in random
       and pattern test file).  When the file is copied back from the
       destination, another file using the same file name (machine ID) but
       with file extension ".bac" is created to store the coming-back file.

   5.6 Since the accuracy of time that OS/2 (and DOS) API reports only up to
       hundred of a second.  The elapse time of a file copy reported by the
       OS/2 API can be zero. When this happen, the performance number of the 
       file copy will not display on the screen.

   5.7 Return Code from CCtest.
       CCtest returns 1 if any error happened, otherwise it returns 0.



6. QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS.


   6.1 Why sometimes CCtest doesn't display performance number ?

      Since the accuracy of time that OS/2 (and DOS) API reports only up to
      hundred of a second.  The elapse time of a file copy reported by the
      OS/2 API can be zero. When this happen, the performance number of the 
      file-copy will not be displayed on the screen.

   6.2 Sometimes, CCtest reports file-compare error but files are identical,
       why ?

      Data got corrupted during transfer in file-compare.

   6.3 It's a known bug in either 3+Open Server or DOS station that generate
       the "incorrect device type" error, how do I solve this problem in order
       to continue to use CCtest ?

      Use -v switch to disable VIO ouput.

   6.4 How do I interpret the performance number reported by CCtest ?

      Two factors in CCtest will affect the performance; file size and buffer
      size, especially when the file size or buffer size is a small number.
      CCtest honestly tells you the performance for each file-transfer, but 
      when transfer size is relatively small, the performance will be
      affected by overhead generated by the OS/2 or DOS API. So, to compare
      the performance for two different protocols or two different machines,
      use the same file size range and buffer size range in testing. Also, 
      since there are too many factors that will contribute to the
      performance, only the average performance number is a fair number for
      your network setup.  The performance for each pass of copy-and-compare
      only tells you the "current condition" of the network, the "current
      condition" might be: server is busy, network traffic is high, and
      ......

   6.5 Is CCtest good enough to be a "performance test" ?

      Yes and No. If you are testing a file system, since CCtest only uses
      sequential read and sequential write to a file, you should use other
      tests like "pf" which can do random read and random write to measure
      the overall performance.  If you are testing protocols, the answer
      is YES if you could correctly interpret the performance numbers
      reported by CCtest.

   6.6 Can I put CCtest in a batch file ?

      Yes, CCtest will return the failure number 1 upon exit, an IF 
      ERRORLEVEL command can then be used to test the return code from
      CCtest and jump to the corresponding error handling routine.  

   6.7 How many destinations can be displayed on the CCtest window ?

      Only the first 10 desinations will be displayed on the CCtest
      window, even the number of destination is larger than ten.

   6.8 How do I save the compare-failed files on disk in over-night testing?

      Since CCtest will not save the compare-failed file on disk when it
      is running in Continue mode (-c switch), an alternative is to write
      a batch file to invoke CCtest several times, this way you can run
      each individual CCtest in Break mode and save the compare-failed
      files on disk. In order to keep all the files, remember to use 
      different Machine ID for each CCtest.

   6.9 The meaning of "destination" are:

      1. A drive name, for example, c:.
      2. A directory, for example, c:\test\cctest.
      3. A network share name, for example, \\saturn\test.
      4. A directory under the network share name, for example, 
         \\saturn\test\copytest\cctest\test1.

  6.10 Why CCtest was designed to copy and compare files on both directions?
       
      Try to excercise both the send and receive routines in protocol stacks.


